  {
    "role": "ops",
    "task_id": "ops-diagnose-restart-race",
    "task_title": "Diagnose the three compounding defects in the restart race",
    "model_id": "grok-4.5",
    "model_label": "Grok 4.5",
    "lane": "hermes",
    "adapterType": "hermes",
    "effort": "cli_default",
    "ok": true,
    "error": null,
    "output": "Root cause \u2014 three compounding defects\n\n1) Restart race / incomplete reclamation\nlaunchd KeepAlive re-execs the start script as soon as pnpm dev exits, while the old embedded-Postgres postmaster and a detached workspace-runtime (:13100) still hold postmaster.pid and the port. The script only frees :3100 and has no single-instance lock, so the new boot collides with leftovers.\nFix: Single-instance lock + wait-and-reclaim of owned ports/PIDs (Postgres + runtime) before exec; do not start until free.\n\n2) Non-draining shutdown\nOn SIGTERM the server exits without draining in-flight runs or signaling detached children, so work and children outlive the parent and become orphans that block the next boot and stay \u201crunning\u201d in DB.\nFix: Shutdown handler: stop accepting work \u2192 SIGTERM process groups \u2192 grace \u2192 SIGKILL stragglers \u2192 then exit.\n\n3) Eager false orphan reap\nStartup calls reapOrphanedRuns() with stale-threshold 0, so every still-running row is immediately process_lost\u2014even live work from the prior instance or a racing restart.\nFix: Reap only after a real stale threshold (and/or heartbeat/PID liveness); never mark process_lost at t=0 on boot.\n\nTogether: unclean exit \u2192 sticky children \u2192 hostile relaunch \u2192 mass false process_lost.",
    "model_reported": "grok-4.5",
